@metadata
title = Records
summary = Typed blocks a program can read without parsing prose. The reason XTXT exists.
@endmetadata

# Records

This is the part that is not Markdown with extra steps.

## The shape

A block whose payload is `Key: value` lines:

@code(language="text")
@task
Title: Ship the reference parser
Status: In Progress
Owner: Subbu
Due: 2026-08-15
@endtask
@endcode

Rendered as a card:

@task
Title: Ship the reference parser
Status: In Progress
Owner: Subbu
Due: 2026-08-15
@endtask

And returned by `xtxt extract` as data:

@code(language="json")
{"title": "Ship the reference parser", "status": "In Progress",
 "owner": "Subbu", "due": "2026-08-15", "done": false, "line": 3}
@endcode

## Why this matters

Consider the same information in Markdown:

@code(language="text")
We're currently working on shipping the reference parser — Subbu
is on it, should be done by the 15th.
@endcode

Everything a program needs is there, and none of it is reachable. Extracting it
means writing a parser for English and being wrong sometimes. The XTXT version
is not better prose; it is prose plus a declaration.

## The standard types

@table
Type | For
-----|----
`@task` | A unit of work
`@decision` | A choice and its reasoning
`@knowledge` | A fact worth keeping
`@note` | An aside or admonition
`@ai` | Model output kept with its context
`@prompt` | A prompt worth reusing
`@chat` | A conversation turn
@endtable

None of these is special to the parser. They are named in the specification so
that independent tools agree on the common cases.

## Inventing your own

@code(language="text")
@experiment
Hypothesis: attention improves recall on long inputs
Dataset: wikitext-103
Result: confirmed, +4.2 points
@endexperiment
@endcode

That works with no parser change and no registration. A reader that does not
know `@experiment` still returns its fields, because the format guarantees the
*shape* is preserved and reported.

## Field names mean nothing to the format

XTXT guarantees that `Priority: High` is a field named Priority with the value
High, in that position. What Priority *means* is your application's business.

That is the same bargain HTML made with class names, and it is why a new record
type works without anyone updating a parser.

@note
Title: The honest cost
Nothing declares that Status is an enum, or that Due is a date. Two authors can
produce two vocabularies that do not line up, and a program consuming both has
to reconcile them itself. Schemas are the most requested missing feature and
are covered on the Limitations page.
@endnote

## Multi-line values and body text

A line without a colon is kept as part of the block's payload text, so a record
can carry prose as well as fields:

@code(language="text")
@decision
Title: Unknown directives are warnings, never errors
Why: A reader from today must stay useful on a document written tomorrow.
@enddecision
@endcode

## Checklists are records too

`- [ ]` items are returned by `extract` as tasks alongside `@task` blocks, so a
throwaway checklist and a formal record both reach a program without you having
to choose upfront.
